{"variants":[{"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}],"paths":["\/documentation\/decisiondiagrams\/zerosuppresseddecisiondiagram"]}],"relationshipsSections":[{"kind":"relationships","identifiers":["doc:\/\/DecisionDiagrams\/s8SendableP","doc:\/\/DecisionDiagrams\/s16SendableMetatypeP"],"title":"Conforms To","type":"conformsTo"}],"sections":[],"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"declarations","declarations":[{"tokens":[{"kind":"attribute","text":"@frozen"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"ZeroSuppressedDecisionDiagram"}],"languages":["swift"],"platforms":["Linux"]}]},{"kind":"content","content":[{"text":"Overview","type":"heading","level":2,"anchor":"overview"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Elements are tested in index order, 0 nearest the root. The table never holds two vertices"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"that test the same element with the same children, nor a vertex whose high child is the empty"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"family, so each family has one vertex: two "},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/DecisionDiagrams\/documentation\/DecisionDiagrams\/ZeroSuppressedDecisionDiagram\/Family","type":"reference","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" values are equal exactly when they hold"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"the same sets. An element no set of a family contains never appears in its diagram, which keeps"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"the diagrams of sparse families small."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"code":["var diagram = ZeroSuppressedDecisionDiagram(elementCount: 3)","let f = diagram.family([[0, 1], [1, 2], [2]])","diagram.count(of: f)                                  \/\/ 3","let g = diagram.including(2, in: f)                   \/\/ {{1, 2}, {2}}","diagram.minimalSets(of: g) == diagram.family([[2]])   \/\/ true","diagram.join(diagram.single(0), g)                    \/\/ {{0, 1, 2}, {0, 2}}"],"type":"codeListing","syntax":"swift"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Families are built by the mutating methods, which add vertices to the table and leave every"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"existing family as it was; a family belongs to the table it was built in and its copies. The"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"table keeps every vertex it has made."}],"type":"paragraph"}]}],"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/DecisionDiagrams\/documentation\/DecisionDiagrams"]]},"kind":"symbol","identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/DecisionDiagrams\/documentation\/DecisionDiagrams\/ZeroSuppressedDecisionDiagram","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"A shared table of zero-suppressed decision diagrams: families of sets drawn from the elements"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"0 ..< elementCount"},{"type":"text","text":", each a directed acyclic graph whose vertices test one element and branch"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"to a low child (the sets without it) and a high child (the sets with it, the element removed),"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"down to the constants: the empty family and the family holding only the empty set (Minato,"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"“Zero-Suppressed BDDs for Set Manipulation in Combinatorial Problems”, 1993)."}],"schemaVersion":{"minor":3,"major":0,"patch":0},"metadata":{"title":"ZeroSuppressedDecisionDiagram","externalID":"s:16DecisionDiagrams014ZeroSuppressedA7DiagramV","role":"symbol","symbolKind":"struct","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"ZeroSuppressedDecisionDiagram","kind":"identifier"}],"roleHeading":"Structure","remoteSource":{"fileName":"ZeroSuppressedDecisionDiagram.swift","url":"https:\/\/github.com\/Tyler-Keith-Thompson\/GrafluentCollections\/blob\/main\/Sources\/DecisionDiagrams\/ZeroSuppressedDecisionDiagram.swift#L26"},"modules":[{"name":"DecisionDiagrams"}],"fragments":[{"kind"